        'cover' => 'covers/short-golden-touch.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'King Midas prizes gold above all, even above his little daughter Marygold, so when a smiling stranger grants his wish that everything he touched became gold, the gift proves disastrous. This retelling of Hawthorne\'s Wonder Book classic carries Midas from gilded roses to a golden child and back again.',
        'text' => "Once upon a time, in the old days when wonders were common, there lived a very rich king named Midas. He had more gold than any other ruler on earth, yet he cared for nothing in the world so much.\n\nHe had one little daughter whom he called Marygold, and whom he loved almost as dearly as his treasure. Almost, but not quite. He liked to say that the finest gift he could leave her was the greatest heap of shining coin the world had ever seen. When she brought him buttercups from the garden he would laugh and say, \"Poh, poh, child! If these flowers were really as golden as they look, they would be worth the picking.\"\n\nIt had not always been so. In his youth the king had loved flowers and music, and his garden held the sweetest roses anyone had ever seen. But now nothing pleased him that could not be weighed in yellow metal, and the only music left to his ears was the chink of coin against coin.\n\nEvery day Midas went down alone into a dark room beneath his palace where his wealth was kept. There he would sift gold dust through his fingers and count his coins piece by piece, whispering happily to himself, \"O Midas, rich King Midas, what a happy man you are!\"\n\nOne morning, while he stood admiring his heaps, a shadow fell across the gold, and a stranger stood smiling in the sunbeam. He was a young man with so bright a face that his very smile seemed edged with golden light. Midas knew he had locked the door behind him, so he understood at once that this visitor must be one of those powerful beings who wandered among people in the old days, granting favors or playing tricks. And because the stranger looked so kind and good-humored, Midas felt sure he had come to grant a wish.\n\n\"You are certainly a wealthy man, friend Midas,\" said the visitor, looking round the room. \"I doubt whether any other four walls on earth hold as much gold as you have piled up here.\"\n\n\"Pretty well, pretty well,\" answered Midas with a sigh. \"But after all it took me my whole life to gather it. A man might live a thousand years before he could grow truly rich!\"\n\n\"What!\" cried the stranger. \"Then all this does not satisfy you?\"\n\nMidas shook his head.\n\n\"Tell me, then,\" said the visitor, greatly amused, \"what would satisfy you? I ask merely for the curiosity of the thing.\"\n\nKing Midas thought hard, feeling sure that this being had power to give whatever he should ask. At last a bright idea came to him, and lifting his head he looked the stranger straight in the face.\n\n\"It is only this,\" he said. \"I am tired of scraping my riches together coin by coin, and finding the heap so small when I have done my best. I wish everything that I touch to be changed to gold!\"\n\nThe stranger's smile grew so broad that the whole room seemed to fill with sunshine. \"The Golden Touch!\" he exclaimed. \"You deserve great credit, friend Midas, for thinking of so brilliant a wish. But are you quite sure it will satisfy you?\"\n\n\"How could it fail?\" said Midas.\n\n\"And will you never be sorry that you own it?\"\n\n\"What could make me sorry? It is everything I have ever wanted.\"\n\n\"Then be it as you wish,\" said the stranger, waving his hand in farewell. \"Tomorrow at sunrise you will find yourself gifted with the Golden Touch.\" His figure suddenly grew so bright that Midas shut his eyes, and when he opened them again the visitor had vanished.\n\nThat night the king scarcely slept for joy. Before the sun was fairly risen he stretched out of bed and touched a chair beside him, but nothing happened, and he began to fear the whole thing had been only a dream. While he lay there feeling wretched, the first ray of morning sun crept through the window and fell across his white bed-linen. Wherever the light rested, the linen turned into a cloth of purest, brightest gold! The Golden Touch had come to him with the first sunbeam.\n\nUp sprang Midas in a joyful frenzy. He seized the bed-post, and it became a fluted golden pillar. He caught at the window curtain, and its tassel grew heavy in his hand, a lump of solid gold. He picked up a book from the table, and at his touch its pages turned into thin plates of gold, with every word grown illegible. Even his clothes became a suit of gold cloth, soft enough to wear though heavy on his shoulders.\n\nLast of all he drew out his handkerchief, which little Marygold had hemmed for him with her own careful stitches. That too turned to gold, her tiny stitches running along the border in threads of gold. For some reason this did not please King Midas at all. He would rather have kept the handkerchief just as she had given it.\n\nThen he put on his spectacles, and found he could not see through them at all, for they had become two little plates of yellow metal.\n\n\"No matter,\" said the king to himself. \"We cannot expect great good fortune without some small inconvenience. My own eyes will do well enough, and soon Marygold will be old enough to read to me.\"\n\nDownstairs he went, laughing to see the staircase rail turn to burnished gold under his hand, and out into the garden, where the roses stood in their fullest bloom. Their blushes and sweetness had delighted everyone who ever saw them, but now Midas knew a way to make them far more precious. Going from bush to bush he exercised his magic touch until every flower and every bud had been changed to gold. He never noticed that the roses kept neither their color nor their fragrance, and that even the little worms inside them turned to metal. By the time this work was done breakfast was waiting, and he hurried back to the palace in high spirits.\n\nAt the sound of crying in the passageway his heart sank. Marygold came slowly in, sobbing as if her heart would break, holding out one of the roses her father had gilded that very morning.\n\n\"How now, my little lady?\" cried Midas. \"What is the matter on this bright morning?\"\n\n\"Oh dear father,\" wept the child, \"this rose is not beautiful! It is the ugliest flower that ever grew! Early this morning I ran into the garden to gather fresh roses for you, because I know you love them best when your own little daughter picks them. But every rose that smelled so sweetly has turned blighted and yellow like this one, and there is no smell left in any of them!\"\n\n\"Poh, my dear child, don't cry over a trifle,\" said Midas quickly. Here indeed he felt rather ashamed, for he had not the courage to confess that he himself had spoiled her flowers. \"A golden rose will last hundreds of years. You can easily trade it for a common one that would wither in a day.\"\n\n\"I don't care for such roses!\" cried Marygold, tossing it away. \"It has no smell, and its hard petals prick my nose!\"\n\nShe sat down sadly to her bread and milk. King Midas meanwhile poured himself a cup of coffee and lifted a spoonful to his lips. The instant it touched them the coffee hardened into a golden lump!\n\n\"Ha!\" exclaimed Midas, rather alarmed.\n\n\"What is the matter, father?\" asked Marygold.\n\n\"Nothing, child, nothing. Eat your milk before it grows cold.\"\n\nSecretly dismayed, he tried a little trout from his plate, touching its tail with one finger. To his horror the fried fish stiffened into a metal fish, its bones golden wires and its fins thin plates of gold. A pretty ornament truly, but a poor breakfast! He broke a hot cake, and it was gold. He took up a boiled egg, and it was gold too. Such a costly meal lay before him, and nothing on the table he could eat! At last, half starving, he snatched up a roasted potato and crammed it hastily into his mouth. But the Golden Touch was quicker than he was. His mouth filled not with soft mealy potato but with hard metal which burned his tongue, and King Midas leaped up roaring with pain, dancing and stamping about the room.\n\n\"Father, dear father!\" cried Marygold, running to him. \"Have you burned your mouth? What is the matter?\"\n\n\"Ah, dear child,\" groaned the poor king, \"I don't know what is to become of your father!\"\n\nAll that richest breakfast, worth its weight in gold, and absolutely good for nothing! Midas began to wonder whether riches were really the most desirable thing in the world, but the thought soon passed, and he sat groaning over his useless splendor until his hunger drew another cry from him.\n\nLittle Marygold could bear it no longer. Whatever the trouble was, she meant to comfort her father. She ran from her chair and threw her arms lovingly about his knees, and King Midas bent down and kissed her forehead. Never had her love seemed worth a thousand times more than all the gold he owned.\n\n\"My precious, precious Marygold!\" he cried.\n\nBut Marygold made no answer.\n\nAlas for the Golden Touch! The moment his lips met her forehead the change began. Her rosy face turned a glittering yellow, with tear-drops of gold standing on her cheeks. Her soft little form grew hard and rigid within her father's arms. When Midas looked at her, trembling with horror, his darling was a human child no longer. The truest treasure in all that room had been his daughter's love, and now Marygold herself was a golden statue.\n\nThere she stood before him, the tender questioning look still fixed upon her face, and the little dimple he loved remaining in her golden chin. Midas had often been fond of saying that the child was worth her weight in gold. Now the saying had come terribly true, and he would gladly have given every bar and coin he owned for one rosy lock of her brown hair.\n\nIn the midst of his despair he looked up, and there by the door stood the same smiling figure that had granted him this disastrous gift.\n\n\"Well, friend Midas,\" said the stranger, \"how do you succeed with the Golden Touch?\"\n\n\"Miserably,\" answered Midas. \"Gold is not everything, and I have lost all that my heart really cared for.\"\n\n\"Ah! Then you have learned something since yesterday. Let us put it to the test. Which is worth more, the gift of the Golden Touch, or one cup of clear cold water?\"\n\n\"A cup of water! Oh blessed water!\" cried Midas. \"I shall never taste it cool and sweet again!\"\n\n\"The Golden Touch, or a crust of bread?\"\n\n\"A crust of bread is worth all the gold on earth,\" said Midas.\n\n\"The Golden Touch, or your own little Marygold, warm and soft and loving as she was an hour ago?\"\n\n\"Oh, my child, my child!\" wept the king. \"I would not give one small dimple in her chin for the power to change this whole wide earth into a lump of gold!\"\n\n\"You are wiser than you were, King Midas,\" said the stranger gravely. \"Your heart is not wholly changed from flesh into gold, or you would be beyond all help. Tell me, do you truly desire to be rid of this gift?\"\n\n\"It is hateful to me!\" cried Midas. A fly settled lightly on his nose, and instantly dropped to the floor a speck of gold, and the king shuddered.\n\n\"Go then,\" said the stranger, \"and plunge into the river that glides past the bottom of your garden. Fill the vase you carry with its water, and sprinkle it freely over anything you wish to change back again from gold into its natural substance. Do this earnestly and sincerely, and it may undo the mischief your greed has wrought.\" No sooner were the words spoken than the lustrous stranger vanished from the room.\n\nWhat a race King Midas ran to the riverside! He snatched up a great pitcher as he went, though alas, it turned to gold the moment he laid hold of it. Without stopping to pull off his shoes he plunged headlong into the stream, and felt the cold blessed water wash the terrible gift away. When he dipped his pitcher to fill it, it gladdened his eyes by changing back into honest brown earthenware. A weight seemed lifted from his own breast too, as if his heart, which had been slowly hardening toward metal, had softened once more into living flesh. On the bank grew a single violet. He touched it gently with his finger, and the flower kept its purple hue. The curse of the Golden Touch had left him.\n\nBack he hastened to the palace carrying water more precious to him now than an ocean of molten gold, and sprinkled it by handfuls over his golden child. The moment the drops touched her, the rosy color stole back into Marygold's cheeks, and the next instant she sneezed and sputtered and started up dripping wet, wondering why her father kept pouring water on her nice dry frock! Of the long hours when she had stood a statue she remembered nothing at all.\n\nNot a word did the king say of his foolishness. Instead he led his little daughter into the garden and sprinkled all the rest of the river water over the ruined rose-bushes, with such happy effect that above five thousand roses opened again in their first beauty and sweetness.\n\nYet the Golden Touch left two marks behind it as long as Midas lived. Ever afterward the sands along that river sparkled like grains of gold, and little Marygold's hair carried a new golden tinge, brighter and richer than before. Her father thought the change a great improvement.\n\nAnd when King Midas grew old, and trotted Marygold's children on his knee, he loved to tell them this marvelous story. But of all other gold in the world, from that day forth, he hated the very sight.",
